    "['class [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'username client001 privilege 1 password 0 encrypted-password', 'parent_command': ['username client001 privilege 15 secret 5 $abc123'], 'root': 'username client001 privilege 15 secret 5 $abc123', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco IOS command for the Juniper user configuration:\\n\\n##\\nusername client001 privilege 1 password 0 encrypted-password\\nusername client002 privilege 1 password 0 encrypted-password\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, \"privilege 1\" corresponds to Juniper\\'s \"operator\" class (low privilege level)\\n2. You would need to replace \"encrypted-password\" with the actual encrypted password string\\n3. The \"0\" before the password indicates that what follows is an already encrypted password (similar to Juniper\\'s format)\\n4. Cisco doesn\\'t have exact class names matching Juniper, but privilege levels serve a similar purpose', 'trans_template': 'username client001 privilege 1 password 0 encrypted-password'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'username client002 privilege 1 password 0 encrypted-password', 'parent_command': ['username client001 privilege 15 secret 5 $abc123'], 'root': 'username client001 privilege 15 secret 5 $abc123', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco IOS command for the Juniper user configuration:\\n\\n##\\nusername client001 privilege 1 password 0 encrypted-password\\nusername client002 privilege 1 password 0 encrypted-password\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, \"privilege 1\" corresponds to Juniper\\'s \"operator\" class (low privilege level)\\n2. You would need to replace \"encrypted-password\" with the actual encrypted password string\\n3. The \"0\" before the password indicates that what follows is an already encrypted password (similar to Juniper\\'s format)\\n4. Cisco doesn\\'t have exact class names matching Juniper, but privilege levels serve a similar purpose', 'trans_template': 'username client